# CORRECTION, 01:42Z. Two entries above were wrong, and the reason both were
# wrong is the same: this ledger was started retrospectively, after arm 1, and
# a retrospective contamination ledger is reconstruction rather than record.
#
#  - A "deploywatch" line claimed 2 requests to /llms.txt and 1 to
#    /api/machines/ask. That command polled /api/health in a loop and made
#    neither. It was written by the command itself on an assumption about what
#    it had done. A fabricated entry in a contamination ledger is worse than a
#    missing one: it subtracts real visitor traffic.
#  - The 01:14:20 line claimed two full prechecks. One refused early on a stale
#    revision and never reached those calls.
#
# UNRECONCILED, and left that way rather than explained away. In hour 496969
# the server recorded 5 requests to /api/machines/ask; three are accounted for
# above. No visitor in arms 1, 2 or 3 requested any /api/machines/* path — that
# is verified against the tool-call inputs of all three transcripts, not against
# their summaries. So the residual is operator-originated or external, and this
# ledger cannot say which.
#
# It does not touch any primary outcome: `declared` rests on
# /api/machines/declaration, which recorded 0 requests, with 0 in the ledger and
# 0 in every transcript. But it is the honest state of the count, and the
# lesson is the one this correction exists to record — only a ledger kept from
# the first request is worth subtracting.
